  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 828 PM MDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nBegin LAST transmission:\n\nThe high pressure aloft is now centered directly over South Central \nNew Mexico, and that was evident today with another large \nsignificant reduction in shower and storm activity across the \nlowlands and Sacramento mountains. Looking at both surface \nobservations and tonight EPZ upper-air sounding, the atmosphere has \nbecome much drier at all levels, and warmer aloft, both adding to \natmospheric stabilization. Over EPZ, the surface dewpoint has \ndropped low enough, that even with a 100 degree max temperature, \nsurface parcels could not break through warm capping layers aloft.\nThe deeper and better moisture is now pushed to the Gila, with much \ndrier air now settled in across the other 80% of the forecast area. \n\nIf you believe the GFS and ECMWF models, they think today was as dry \nas it will get through the forecast period, with daily minimal \nincreases in moisture, from the north (of all places) going forward. \nAnd that looks reasonable, given the pattern and position of the \nhigh aloft. The slowly returning moisture, will be a repeat visitor \n,from the same moisture the upper high shoved out, and has now spun \nW, N, E, and back south into New Mexico, on the anticyclonic flow \naround the high pressure centered over NM. This should mean slightly \nbetter chances Friday for some convection over the Sacs, and \ncontinued storms over the Gila region. We will see a shift in storm \nmotion tomorrow, from SE to NW to a motion from NE to SW. Despite \nthe small increases in moisture, most of the lowlands will still \nhave minimal rain/storm chances, with another day of lowland 100 \ndegree max temps.\n\nThrough the weekend, we a mix of moist and dry air over the forecast \narea, trapped under the high, and recycled each heat cycle into \nscattered mountain and isolated (at best) lowland showers and \nstorms. We will likely struggle to get widespread airmass \ndestabilization with the high overhead, and are looking to the east \nfor the presence of any approaching disturbance to enhance \nrain/storm chances. Former TS Bertha passes to our south, but so far \nsouth we don't look to get any moisture or dynamic help over our \narea.\n\nNext week doesn't look real good for MOST of the Borderland, if you \nare hoping for more rain, clouds, and cooler temperatures. The upper \nhigh remains locked in over the Southern Rockies to Southern Plains, \nwith the bulk of any monsoon moisture plume concentrated to the west \nover AZ and W NM. Thus, it appears shower/storm activity will be \nslight chance at best over our eastern and central areas, and \nscattered over the Bootheel/Gila areas.\nTemperatures look to remain quite warm, with 100 degree daily highs \ncontinuing across the lowlands.\n\nEnd LAST transmission:  BIRD OUT",
